 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T08:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**Yes, you should strongly consider staying indoors in Kuala Lumpur today.** \n\nAir quality readings in parts of KL currently fall into the **Unhealthy** to **Very Unhealthy** range (with elevated levels of fine particulate matter like PM2.5). For someone with asthma, breathing air with high pollution levels can irritate your airways, trigger coughing, wheezing, shortness of breath, or provoke a severe asthma attack.\n\nIf you must go outside, or to protect yourself while indoors, keep these precautions in mind:\n\n* **Keep windows and doors closed:** Minimize how much outdoor polluted air enters your living space. If you have air conditioning, run it on a recirculate setting if possible.\n* **Have your medication ready:** Ensure your rescue inhaler (reliever) is close at hand at all times. Follow your prescribed asthma action plan meticulously.\n* **Wear a mask if you must go out:** If going outdoors is unavoidable, wearing a tightly fitted respirator mask (such as an N95) can help filter out fine particles, though staying indoors remains the safest option.\n* **Avoid strenuous activity:** Refrain from exercising or doing heavy physical work outdoors, as increased breathing rates cause you to inhale significantly more pollutants.\n\n**When to seek medical help:** \nIf you experience symptoms like chest tightness, persistent coughing, wheezing, or if you find yourself needing your rescue inhaler more frequently than usual—and it isn't providing relief—seek medical attention immediately.",
